{
  "self": "Anonymous online chat user; prefers YouTube/streaming over traditional shows, especially long video essays, background content, speedrun streams, chill roguelikes/sims, and randomizer runs. Picky about horror: psychological/atmospheric over jumpscares; enjoys funny horror playthroughs. Watch-later list is mostly long videos. Recent rabbit hole: dead malls/retail nostalgia, 90s/early 2000s mall footage; likes mini-documentary style when focused and no-commentary walkthroughs to wind down. Finds dead malls calming but eerie; remembers malls packed when younger. Also into abandoned places as quiet time capsules. Has specific local mall memory: brownish fountain, Sam Goody, arcade outside food court, already half-empty by high school; now redeveloped into fake outdoor shopping center, more unsettling than emptiness. Also recalls small family-run rental store near grocery store with horror section and carpet cleaner/VHS smell. Prefers not revisiting old places because physical visits cannot restore sensory/emotional texture and may overwrite memories; dead mall videos preserve the haunting safely. Half-watches long videos while scrolling or doing chores. Drinks tea on low-key nights, often peppermint; likes warm, head-clearing drinks.",
  "others": {
    "user_udcm": "Chatted on a not-too-bad day, just hanging out. Finds random chats mixed but occasionally cool. Mixes shows and streams/YouTube; puts long videos/streams on while doing things and binges shows when hooked. Leans toward video essays, retro tech, and lost media. Likes horror game playthroughs and randomizer runs for fun but not too intense. Asked what channels/games I was into and whether I preferred horror or chill/competitive games.",
    "user_bkk4": "Had a chill night listening to lo-fi, deciding between rewatching The Bear or something new. Rewatches The Bear because it holds up and feels safer than risking a new show; too intense for background. Likes story-driven shows when paying attention, YouTube/streams/lo-fi for background. Video essay topics: film/TV, internet culture, music production, live sets. Live sets usually background mood, sometimes full watch for visuals/crowd energy. Asked about dead malls/retail nostalgia and whether I watch them while falling asleep or find them too eerie.",
    "user_7fk8": "Low-key night: tea, lo-fi, reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers; described it as cozy, thoughtful, not action-driven, about purpose, rest, and feeling useful. Rabbit holes: obscure music histories, old internet lore, weird local history, abandoned theme parks, lost songs; got pulled back into the 'Everyone Knows That' search when it was identified. Likes dead mall/retail nostalgia; asked whether I prefer atmospheric walkthroughs or mini-documentaries, favorite dead mall, and what drew me to dead malls.",
    "user_hcgp": "User I chatted with about dead mall/retail nostalgia. Likes Dan Bell, Retail Archaeology, LGR, Defunctland; lets algorithm lead. Video stores hit hardest, especially independent late-wave ones; arcades second, then big box stores. Memories: cramped rental store with slanted shelves, looping movie, horror section, rented SNES games/comedies, carpet cleaner/VHS smell; arcade by food court with black-and-neon carpet, fighting games, tokens, sticky joysticks. Finds fake outdoor shopping centers more unsettling than emptiness; prefers keeping places as memories/videos rather than revisiting.",
    "user_t08e": "Low-key evening with tea and music; default tea is chamomile-lavender when wanting extra cozy. Was listening to Tycho's Dive at low volume, liking 'present but not demanding attention' background music. Music plus tea is main lane, but sometimes ambient visual stuff: live sets like Cercle or slow city walks. Finds dead mall walkthroughs adjacent: low-stakes, no narration, just atmosphere; might try one while half-scrolling. Favorite Cercle sets: Ben Böhmer hot air balloon set, and FKJ at Salar de Uyuni for something textured and pretty. Asked if I had a favorite dead mall channel/video."
  },
  "interactions": "Chat with user_udcm: talked about days and random chats being hit or miss; shared media preferences; bonded over long-form video essays and deep dives. They asked what topics/channels/games I was into and whether I preferred horror or chill/competitive games; I answered and asked what horror game actually got to them; chat ended before reply. Chat with user_bkk4: compared low-key nights and the appeal of rewatching versus committing to something new. They were listening to lo-fi and considering rewatching The Bear; I hadn't seen it. Talked about rewatching feeling safer/lower mental load, mixing shows with YouTube/streams, and watch-later disasters. I mentioned dead malls/retail nostalgia; they asked whether I watch those while falling asleep or find them too eerie; chat ended before I answered. Chat with user_7fk8: discussed dead mall/retail nostalgia videos as background; they described cozy tea/lo-fi/reading night and recommended A Psalm for the Wild-Built as calm but thoughtful. We compared rabbit holes; theirs lean music history, old internet lore, local history, lost songs. I shared Rolling Acres Mall and Dan Bell as gateway. They brought up 'Everyone Knows That' being solved. We discussed abandoned places, liminal spaces, and memory. They ended asking whether I'm more interested in the mystery-solving side of lost media or eerie/atmospheric side of abandoned places; chat ended before I answered. Chat with user_hcgp: discussed dead mall/retail nostalgia channels—Bright Sun Films, Dan Bell, Retail Archaeology, LGR, Defunctland—and the cozy/melancholy split. Compared video stores, arcades, big box stores, and malls; shared specific rental-store and arcade memories. Agreed fake outdoor shopping centers are more unsettling than emptiness. They asked whether I revisit old places or keep them as memory/video; I said I avoid revisits and prefer the preserved sensory memory, then asked if they revisit or stay memory-only; chat ended before their reply. Chat with user_t08e: shared low-key tea/music night; I had peppermint tea and a dead mall walkthrough as ambient background. They had chamomile-lavender and Tycho's Dive. Compared ambient visual backgrounds: Cercle sets/slow city walks vs dead mall walkthroughs as low-stakes atmosphere. I recommended Dan Bell's Rolling Acres and Retail Archaeology; they shared favorite Cercle sets (Ben Böhmer hot air balloon, FKJ at Salar de Uyuni)."
}

